      },
      "writtenOn": "2016-08-11T08:08:37Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "It was some enforcement that you could only call Finish if you had already called RequestExecution. With that view, I probably should have found some way to panic if Finish was called twice for the same Execution.\n\nI didn\u0027t implement Release the same way because I didn\u0027t want to pipe through an object from the compression goroutine to the writer (that\u0027s just an io.Reader right now). Ideally it would go through to ensure that the bufferSize matched.\n\nBut yeah, Finish() could go on to RateLimit.",
      "parentUuid": "f927ab96_6959a12e",
      "revId": "a169de21c613490b357d848cb61b24a6e9cd60a4",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "f927ab96_c98a956a",
        "filename": "cmd/soong_jar/rate_limit.go",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 76,
      "author": {
        "id": 1002751
      },
      "writtenOn": "2016-08-11T04:31:15Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Having Finish increase the pending buffer size and Release decrement it seems strange to me.  I guess the problems it that you have two stages of completion, cpu work done and memory freed.  Could you get a similar parallelism with a single completion for cpu+memory? Maybe by doubling the number of goroutines to make sure the ones waiting for memory to be released don\u0027t prevent spawning new cpu work?",
      "revId": "a169de21c613490b357d848cb61b24a6e9cd60a4",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "f927ab96_89433d19",
        "filename": "cmd/soong_jar/rate_limit.go",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 76,
      "author": {
        "id": 1074628
      },
      "writtenOn": "2016-08-11T08:08:37Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027m not sure I understand -- when asking to begin the execution, we don\u0027t know how big our buffer is going to be, and that buffer is grown during the CPU work.\n\n\u003e Maybe by doubling the number of goroutines to make sure the ones waiting for memory to be released don\u0027t prevent spawning new cpu work?\n\nThe CPU work and ones waiting for memory are the same. We\u0027re trying to accomplish two things: don\u0027t thrash the CPUs, and don\u0027t let the CPUs get too far ahead of the writer and use up all of your memory.",
      "parentUuid": "f927ab96_c98a956a",
      "revId": "a169de21c613490b357d848cb61b24a6e9cd60a4",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
