    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "f927ab96_499f85a4",
        "filename": "cmd/soong_jar/soong_jar.go",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 243,
      "author": {
        "id": 1002751
      },
      "writtenOn": "2016-08-11T04:31:15Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The structure of this loop is very confusing.  It is effectively using a state machine for flow control by setting channels to nil.\n\nI\u0027d suggest restructuring this as a pipeline (https://blog.golang.org/pipelines).  Have the main thread iterate through files and create structs describing what to do.  The first stage gets descriptor structs and ratelimits them.  It calls functions to start any necessary work, collecting a future if necessary.  The future could take the form of the Reader half of a io.Pipe, and the parallel block compression could wrap the PipeReaders in an io.MultiReader, so the interface is always an io.Reader.\n\nIt puts the futures into a struct and passes it to the 3rd stage.  The 3rd stage gets a futures struct, waits on the futures it needs, and writes to the file.",
      "revId": "a169de21c613490b357d848cb61b24a6e9cd60a4",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "f927ab96_2955e9c5",
        "filename": "cmd/soong_jar/soong_jar.go",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 243,
      "author": {
        "id": 1074628
      },
      "writtenOn": "2016-08-11T08:08:37Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yes, it was a more \"standard\" control flow with embedded for loops, which would have mapped to pipelines by just replacing some of that with goroutines and channels. But then I refactored into this after looking at the advanced go concurrency patterns slides: https://blog.golang.org/advanced-go-concurrency-patterns\n\nio.Pipe isn\u0027t particularly useful here -- it would require another Copy. We need to have buffers, otherwise we can\u0027t parallelize any of this. So we\u0027ll be writing into a buffer as a result of the compression. Currently, that completed buffer is just passed as an io.Reader, and we Copy it in the main thread over to the zip writer. If the compression goroutine wanted to return the reader half of the Pipe, it would need to create the Pipe, then Copy the buffer into the pipe, and stay blocked waiting for a reader. Then the main thread would need to Copy from the pipe into the zip writer. And since the Pipe is in the middle, the second io.Copy can\u0027t use the buffer\u0027s WriteTo implementation, so it will allocate an extra temporary buffer.",
      "parentUuid": "f927ab96_499f85a4",
      "revId": "a169de21c613490b357d848cb61b24a6e9cd60a4",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "f927ab96_e98f5177",
        "filename": "cmd/soong_jar/soong_jar.go",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 427,
      "author": {
        "id": 1002751
      },
      "writtenOn": "2016-08-11T04:31:15Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This seems like more precision on ratelimiting than necessary.  You don\u0027t need to ensure you have exactly as many goroutines as cpus, you just need to make sure that the number of pending files to be zipped doesn\u0027t far outstrip the number of cpus so that you have some chance of finishing the next zip file and freeing its memory in a reasonable amount of time.",
      "revId": "a169de21c613490b357d848cb61b24a6e9cd60a4",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "f927ab96_49d78515",
        "filename": "cmd/soong_jar/soong_jar.go",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 427,
      "author": {
        "id": 1074628
      },
      "writtenOn": "2016-08-11T08:08:37Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "How would you loosen it up? You\u0027d still want to block within this for loop so that 20 1MB files behaved similarly to 1 20MB file, since the compression load is similar.\n\nYou also want compressions that started first not to get delayed by ones that started later in case we are starving the writer, but that\u0027s not a case I\u0027m seeing (but maybe you\u0027d see it on a smaller # of core machine with an SSD or tmpfs).\n\nNot caring about # of CPUs can be tested with this code rather easily (-j9999). When I first implemented this, I saw ~50% performance improvement over no rate limit. Now that it\u0027s also limited based on outstanding buffers, allowing more compression goroutines than cpu threads does speed up total time by a couple percent in my tests, but adds some more variability as well.\n\nIt\u0027s also handy if you want to parallelize your zip file, but not take over your entire machine :) But I suppose that can also be implemented by setting GOMAXPROCS.",
      "parentUuid": "f927ab96_e98f5177",
      "revId": "a169de21c613490b357d848cb61b24a6e9cd60a4",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
